            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

The air leakage (and therefore heat loss) from your home is much higher that it should be. While operating the blower door &amp; using the infrared camera, we found a few key locations where this air leakage occurs:



- Through all the openings (hallway, bedroom &amp; living room ceilings &amp; walls) for that Enviro whole house ventilation system (that has been turned off for years). It didn't sound like you had been made aware of this system when you purchased the house?



- Both bathroom exhaust fans appear to allow heat loss even when the fans are not operating. You may want to consider upgrading these fans to new Energy Star models that have good butterfly dampers, use less electricity &amp; operate alot quieter.



- There was a very strange air leakage location above the ceiling of the front main floor (living &amp; dining room), where outside air is making its' way into the header space between the main floor &amp; the second floor living area above. This cold air coming in has been causing some dark spots to form on your ceilings. To fix this, you will need to gain access into that small attic space in the front section above the main floor (likely easiest to cut an access in through the short wall in a bedroom closet upstairs) so that all of those header spaces along the bottom of that short 2nd floor wall can be fully sealed &amp; insulated. I will email you some information about this.



- The windows &amp; both doors are sealed reasonably well, however please ensure the weatherstripping and door sweeps for the doors are regularly updated.



- Some other smaller air leakage in your house is from around &amp; through many of the electrical outlets &amp; wall switches located on exterior walls. For these electrical outlets, you can install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 269&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x6 construction with R20 fibreglass batt insulation. For your longer term planning, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to add 3" (R15) of rigid foam insulation to the exterior of these walls (including the short walls of that tank room below the house) in conjunction with a future exterior siding upgrade.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of the house up to a minimum R35 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 937&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;22.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <windows />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



As discussed while I was at your house, the Arctic Energy Alliance always recommends homeowners complete all energy conservation (eg: LED Bulbs, timer switches, etc.) &amp; energy efficiency (eg: heating &amp; hot water systems) measures before investing in renewable energy generation systems.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the front Southwest facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 960&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 per year in a thermal electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation>

Your house has two toilets and one of them is an older 13 litre model, and you should consider replacing it with a lower water consumption model. When replacing toilets, purchase a 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model. Look for a MAP flush rating capacity of a minimum 800 - 100 grams per flush !!



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing a toilet with a new 4.8 Liter or less (or dual-flush) model, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save ~$50 &lt;/b&gt; per person, per year in water and energy savings.  There are currently no rebates available for toilets from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</WaterConservation>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



This house has multiple components comprising the ventilation design:



1. There is a central Enviro "Heat Recovery Ventilation" (HRV) system that is installed in the lower tank room with piping that comes up through the house all the way into the attic. There are vent openings on the main floor &amp; 2nd floor hallway ceilings, as well as various fresh air feeds into the bedrooms &amp; living room. While looking in the attic, there is a large network of ductwork to feed this system.



As newer owners of this house, you were not aware of this ventilation system tucked away in the lower tank room, and it looks as though it has been turned off for quite some time. In fact ........ with the amount of air leakage in your house, plus the other exhaust ventilation in your home .......... the HRV is really not required.



2. Both bathrooms have an exhaust fan, operated by a regular switch. An easy upgrade is to install timer switches (5 -10-15-20 minutes) so the fans will be operated long enough to exhaust odours &amp; humidity, however cannot be accidentally left on all day, wasting large amounts of heated air from your house.



3. The kitchen has a range hood fan that does not vent to the outside. If you did want it to vent to the outside, it looks like there is room to run ductwork out &amp; through the back wall of the house. When is is time to replace it, install an Energy Star rated fan that has a good butterfly damper (to prevent excess heat loss when the fan is not operating), uses less electricity and has a low noise (sone) level. 



4. The clothes dryer is vented to the outside. There was a small buildup of clothes lint in the exhaust vent (at the outside of the house), so I cleaned it out for you while I was there.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Wood Pellet Stove&lt;/b&gt;



During my visit, we had talked about possibly some supplemental heat for that front living room area. A simple solution is to install a wood pellet stove that has its' own thermostat for the location you are trying to supplement heat into.



If you choose to install a wood pellet stove, the unit should have a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply (just like your current boiler &amp; hot water heater do).



Using this pellet stove to supplement about 50% of the heating for part of the main floor is estimated to create about $691 of estimated annual savings (these savings are net of oil saved &amp; the wood pellets purchased).



&lt;b&gt;Rebates:&lt;/b&gt;



If you were to install an eligible wood pellet stove, you would qualify for &lt;b&gt;1/3 of the purchase cost up to $ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (both bathroom fans, and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.0 Pascal. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment would not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home. As both the boiler &amp; the hot water heater are direct vent units (with a dedicated &amp; sealed outside combustion air supply), they are not at risk of backdrafting. If you install a wood or pellet stove, it should also have a dedicated outside combustion air "sealed" to the stove.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date (normally every 10 years).



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Norman Wells&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.52 per litre 

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.14 per bag



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



